(Example: An inventory management system that tracks stock levels in real time.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Analytical Databases \(OLAP  Online Analytical Processing\):) Tj T*
(These are designed for data analysis and business intelligence. Unlike) Tj T*
(operational databases, they store historical and aggregated data, optimised for) Tj T*
(complex queries and reporting to help businesses gain insights into trends and) Tj T*
(performance.) Tj T*
(Example: A data warehouse that stores years of sales data to identify seasonal) Tj T*
(trends and support strategic planning decisions.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Key Differences:) Tj T*
(- Operational databases handle current data; analytical databases handle) Tj T*
(  historical data.) Tj T*
(- Operational databases are optimised for transactions; analytical databases) Tj T*
(  are optimised for complex queries and reporting.) Tj T*
(- Operational databases support daily operations; analytical databases support) Tj T*
(  decision-making.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(5. Types of Data and Use Cases for NoSQL Databases) Tj T*
(=================================================) Tj T*
(Databases can be classified by their structure into three types of data:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(- Structured data: Highly organised data arranged in a predefined format) Tj T*
(  \(rows and columns\), as in relational databases.) Tj T*
(- Semi-structured data: Has some organisation but lacks the rigidity of) Tj T*
(  structured data. Common formats include XML and JSON.) Tj T*
(- Unstructured data: Has no predefined format and includes various types such) Tj T*
(  as text documents, images, and videos) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(NoSQL databases are most effective in use cases that involve:) Tj T*
(- Large volumes of unstructured or semi-structured data \(e.g., social media) Tj T*
(  posts, user-generated content\)) Tj T*
(- Applications that need high scalability and performance under heavy loads) Tj T*
(  \(e.g., real-time web applications\).) Tj T*
(- Scenarios where the data model evolves rapidly and a flexible schema is) Tj T*
(  needed \(e.g., content management systems, e-commerce catalogues\).) Tj T*
(- Big data and real-time analytics where traditional relational databases) Tj T*
(  would struggle with volume and velocity.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(6. DBMS Without Server Configuration and Its Advantages) Tj T*
(=====================================================) Tj T*
(SQLite is the DBMS that does not require server configuration. It is a) Tj T*
(lightweight, file-based relational database that is self-contained and) Tj T*
(serverless.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Advantages of SQLite) Tj T*
(- No server setup required: It runs entirely within the application, removing) Tj T*
(  the complexity of configuring and maintaining a separate database server.) Tj T*
ET
endstream
