(7. ACID Properties in the Context of a DBMS) Tj T*
(============================================) Tj T*
(ACID stands for Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, and Durability. These are) Tj T*
(the four key properties that relational DBMSs use to ensure data integrity and) Tj T*
(consistency during database transactions.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(- Atomicity: A transaction is treated as a single, indivisible unit. Either) Tj T*
(  all operations within the transaction are completed successfully, or none) Tj T*
(  of them are applied. This prevents partial updates that could corrupt data.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(- Consistency: Every transaction brings the database from one valid state to) Tj T*
(  another. The database must always remain in a consistent state before and) Tj T*
(  after a transaction, ensuring data correctness at all times.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(- Isolation: Transactions occurring simultaneously \(in parallel\) are executed) Tj T*
(  independently of one another. The intermediate state of a transaction is) Tj T*
(  not visible to other transactions, preventing conflicts and ensuring data) Tj T*
(  integrity under concurrent access.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(- Durability: Once a transaction has been committed, the changes are permanent) Tj T*
(  and will persist even in the event of a system crash or failure. The DBMS) Tj T*
(  uses mechanisms such as logs and backups to ensure durability.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Together, ACID properties guarantee that database transactions are complete,) Tj T*
(accurate, independent, and permanent, making relational DBMSs reliable for) Tj T*
(critical data operations.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
ET
